{
  "dom_id": "question-168117",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the best linear approximation of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required linear approximation is $f(x,y)\u2248$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\u2248f(a,b)+\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot \u27e8x-a,y-b\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(1,1)+\u2207f(1,1)\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4.\n$$\n\nAnd from part 3, we know that the gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u2207f(1,1)=\u2207(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=\u27e84,\\,2\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(1,1)+\u2207f(1,1)\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =4+\u27e84,2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =4+4(x-1)+2(y-1) \\\\  & =4x+2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
